  [1mpath[0m. That means clarifying questions one at a time, 2-3 approaches with trade-offs, a sectioned design you approve
  section by section, and a written spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m before any code. The ratchet is one-way — I won't
  quietly drop back to "just edit [38;5;153mapp.js[39m."

  So the original one-line request has resolved into two changes: [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns a server-assigned [38;5;153muserId[39m (not takes
  one), and a new tracking layer records login events. The second is the real project.

  Two things I want on the record before we design, because they constrain it:

  - [1mThis app is a static page with no backend.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is loaded by a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, there's no build step, no
    dependencies, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts. Anything that "persists" has to go somewhere off the page, and
    whatever we choose is the first piece of infrastructure this repo acquires.
  - [1mA login audit trail written by the browser is not trustworthy for security or compliance purposes.[0m The client can be
    modified, events dropped, or events forged. If this is for product analytics ("how many people log in, who's
    active"), client-side is fine and normal. If it's for security or compliance ("who accessed this account, prove
    it"), the record has to be written server-side at the point of authentication, and the browser-side piece is at best
    a convenience copy. This changes what we build, so it's my first question.

  Also worth flagging now, not later: login events tied to a user identifier are personal data under GDPR/CCPA.
  Retention, and whether the ID is pseudonymous, are design decisions we should make deliberately rather than inherit by
  accident. I'll raise these at the right point rather than front-load them.
